m margin; about 49px at a 16px root) and start where the first message will, so the pane does not jump when the conversation arrives: the sender, date and `Loading conversation…` are ONE line (`role="status"` on the status span only); where the loaded reader has a meta row (read controls, read-only, a selection scope) that line IS the meta row's slot, and otherwise it is drawn out of flow over the first placeholder row (`.is-overlay`), which therefore paints no bar of its own. Without a list-row identity the subject slot is a hidden `h2.reader-subject` with a bar inside, so its margins match the real subject by construction. Measured in a browser with a deliberately delayed `getConversation` (fixture, no read controls, 1280×800): before, the first skeleton row was at y=244.3 and the first real message at y=187.9 (+56.4px); after, rows [187.9, 236.9, 285.9] against messages [187.9, 236.9, 285.9] (0.0px), also 0.0px at 1000×700 with a 20px root font (rows 213.2/274.2/335.2) and, as a replica with the meta-row classes and a real-shaped `.reader-meta-row`, 204.1 against 204.1. The rest of these numbers come from the CSS and are asserted by `tests/reader/geometry.test.ts`.
215:   Remaining: the badge counts only what the activity snapshot carries (the same rows Activity shows), not a separate mailbox-wide total. The Contact disclosure stays in the rail below 1000px; it has no Superhuman analogue.
228:   - **Narrow disclosure:** inside the Contact disclosure below 1000px, the same content stacks compactly under the drawer header.
229:   - **No rows, no column (gap 18):** while a ready list has no rows, no reader is open and no reply target exists, there is no conversation to have a contact: the column is not rendered and its grid track goes (`.app-main.contact-collapsed`), so the list takes the width instead of leaving a blank 272px slab; below 1000px the rail's Contact button is absent, so it cannot open an empty drawer. A loading or failed list does not collapse (rows may still arrive); with rows, nothing changes. If focus was inside the contact surface when the last row went, it moves to the empty line.
230:   - **No footer toolbar:** Account, Activity and Drafts stay in the rail, their one home. The contact column does not exist below 1000px and is `inert` while a drawer is open, so footer toggles would vanish on narrow viewports and could not close their own drawer or receive focus back (Drafts is no longer a drawer; the rule holds for Account and Activity).
236:14. **Type scale and dates.** Done (2026-11-07 typeset pass). The shell type is semantic tokens on `:root`: `--type-headline` 1rem, `--type-body` 0.75rem, `--type-count` 0.6875rem, `--type-mono` 0.85rem, weights 400/600 only (`--weight-regular`, `--weight-strong`). `.app-main` sets Body as the shell default, so the rail, contact pane, notices, drawers, the reader chrome and form controls are 12px instead of an inherited 16px or ad hoc 0.8/0.85/0.9/0.95/1.1rem. Shell h2 is Headline; h3/h4 and in-panel drawer headings are Title; the reader subject is Headline at 600. Row dates follow the Label rules below and are deterministic (no default-locale dependence: the old `30 Sept 2001` vs `Sep 30, 2001` rendered-layout gate now passes under any `LANG`). Rows carry `<time datetime>` plus the full date as the title; reader message times are full dates with `datetime`. The rendered larger-text gate expected the compact action disclosure at a 25px root font (was 23px) after this pass, and at 34.5px (expanded at 30px) after the rail/contact pass, since the fixed-px rail and contact pane leave the list wider at every text size. The Remind row action (a fourth 2.667em target) and the attachment glyph then moved it again, and the glyph was found scaling with the text (30px at a 30px root, against the 16px spec above), so `.row-attachment .row-glyph` is now pinned to 16px: at 1000px the expanded row fits up to 25.5px and collapses from about 26px, the compact row fits up to 30.25px (it overflowed by 10px at 30px before the pin), and the gate now steps 24px (expanded, four actions), 28px and 30px (compact, no overflow, disclosure operable). Measured compact-overflow bounds: 1000px above 30.25px (32px overflows by 27px), 1100px above 36.5px, 1280px from 39px, where it first collapses (2px, growing to 5px at 46px). The action glyphs and targets stay em-based on purpose (larger text enlarges them); the sender and subject floors are not traded for more headroom.
256:19. **Reader and composer, rendered follow-up.** Done (2026-11-10, measured in a browser at 1280×800, 1000×700 and a 20px root; the Gmail meta-row variant and the quote toggle on replicas). Each item is described where it lives; this entry is the index. Run notes: `.impeccable/critique/` (run 4).
427:- **Style:** 40px wide on Warm Charcoal, with a 1px Seam on its right. The top action, Inbox, is a 28px Rail Pebble circle centered on the tab line. The other destination glyphs sit in a 30px Inkwell pill at a 28px pitch, with the first glyph on the first list line. Utilities (Account, Activity, Drafts, and Contact below 1000px) are ghost glyphs at the bottom. The active destination glyph is Paper White on a 24px Rail Pebble disc; the rest are Graphite.
461:- **Thread above the card:** the card shrinks the thread scroller, so the reader scrolls the message being answered (the latest) fully into view when a reply opens and keeps it there as the card grows (Cc / Subject), by scrolling only; focus never moves. It stops following as soon as the user scrolls (wheel, touch, click) or moves through the thread with J/K or the arrows. A latest message taller than the strip is aligned by its top. Measured (fixture, latest message 166px): the thread strip was 301px with Cc / Subject closed and 241px open at 1280×800, and the latest message showed 162.5px then 102.5px of its 166px (hidden behind the card); now 166px of 166px in both states (scrollTop 9.5 and 69.5), at 1000×700 (strip 201 and 191px) too, and at a 20px root (strip 225.6px, message 207.3px fully visible). The 60% cap is kept and no extra minimum was needed: it leaves at least 40% of the pane.

